---
globs: src/**/*.ts, wrangler.jsonc
alwaysApply: false
---

- `src/index.ts` holds the page markup, the `POST /hi` handler, and the `scheduled` cron handler.
  Keeping the Worker in one file is intentional; don't split it up as a drive-by refactor.
- Bindings come from the generated `CloudflareBindings` interface. After editing bindings or vars in
  `wrangler.jsonc`, run `npm run cf-typegen` or the typecheck will be wrong.
- Anything slow belongs in `c.executionCtx.waitUntil(...)` so the user gets their response
  immediately. Work in there must catch its own errors and log them — the response has already been
  sent, so a throw is invisible to the user and to the request lifecycle.
- Validation failures return status **422** with a bare HTML fragment (`<p class="hint">…</p>`), not
  JSON. htmx swaps the fragment into `#result`, so a JSON body would render as literal text.
- The `stamp` class on the success fragment is a contract with `public/draw.js`, which watches
  `#result` for it to start the success animation. Renaming it breaks that silently.
- `GET /drawings` is public and has no moderation gate, so any query change there changes what the
  world can see. It filters on `drawing_key`, not on `status` — don't assume `status` limits it.
- Log with `console.log(JSON.stringify({ event: "snake_case_name", ... }))`. Reuse the existing
  event names where one fits.
- `scheduled()` must keep its `ENVIRONMENT !== 'production'` early return. It is what stops PR
  preview Workers from emailing real people.
- Preview and production share one D1 database and one R2 bucket. Treat every write path as
  production data.
- The `preview` environment in `wrangler.jsonc` inherits everything you add at the top level. It
  already has to blank out `routes` and `crons` for that reason, so when you add a top-level key
  ask whether preview needs an override too.

See @AGENTS.md for the full submission lifecycle and the `status` values.
